428 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (We explain the tuned LightGBM with two methods. SHAP \(TreeExplainer, 2,000 test samples\) splits each prediction into feature contributions. We sum them per original variable, since one-hot encoding splits each categorical variable into many columns. Permutation importance \(8,000 test stays, 5 repeats\) measures the PR-AUC drop when a raw column is shuffled. SHAP shows what the model uses; permutation shows what its performance depends on.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
429 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (Both methods rank past inpatient stays and discharge destination first and second, far ahead of everything else, so this result is robust. Below them, permutation drops are under 0.01, and their order is not reliable. n_prior_visits ranks 3rd for SHAP but cannot appear in the permutation ranking because it is built inside the Pipeline. The ablation pointed the same way: the feature adds no new information but gathers the prior-care signal into one readable variable.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
430 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (Long stays keep a high raw readmission rate in the EDA \(12.7% at 14 days\), yet SHAP gives them a negative effect. SHAP measures each effect given the other features: long stays already carry many diagnoses and medications. No top variable is known until after discharge, which is a further check against leakage.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
