486 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (A model is calibrated if its scores match real observed frequencies. Here, among patients who received a score of 0.3, about 30% should really be readmitted. Since a hospital could use this score as a risk level \(for example, with an obligation to call every patient above a certain threshold\), we checked the calibration of the retained model \(LightGBM\) on the test set. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
487 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (To do so, we used 3 tools : )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
488 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1144 0 R
/E (Reliability curve: the test stays are split into 10 groups of equal size \(about 1970 stays each\) by predicted score. For each group, we compared the mean score with the observed readmission rate \(Figure 1 : Reliability curve and distribution of predicted scores before calibration\). )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
489 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1145 0 R
/E (Brier score: the mean squared error between the score and the real outcome \(0 being perfect\). As a reference, always predicting the base rate gives 0.0987. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
490 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1146 0 R
/E (ECE: average gap between the mean score and the observed rate over the 10 groups, weighted by group size. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
491 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (Figure 1 shows that the row scores are clearly miscalibrated. Their mean is 0.434, while only 11.1% of the test stays are readmitted. Plus, the distribution histogram is centered around 0.4. The reliability curve lies below the diagonal over the whole range \(for example, stays with a score around 0.7 are readmitted only 29% of the time\). The Brier score \(=0.2051\) is even worse than the base-rate reference \(0.0987\). The ECE is 0.323. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
492 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
493 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (To recalibrate the raw scores, we compared Platt scaling and isotonic regression. Both were fitted on the out-of-fold scores of the train set, and the choice was made on the train set only. Using the same 5 folds as before, each method was fitted on four folds and evaluated on the fifth. Our rule was to keep the simpler recalibration method unless isotonic regression was clearly better. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
494 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (The two methods were almost identical \(Brier = 0.09632 for Platt and = 0.09639 for isotonic, isotonic being better in only 1 fold out of 5\). So we kept Platt scaling. The test set was then used to check this choice \(Figure 2: Reliability curves and score distribution of the raw, isotonic, and Platt scores\) :)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
495 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1147 0 R
/E (The mean probability \(11.6%\) now matches the real readmission rate \(11.1%\), and the reliability curve follows the diagonal)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
496 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1148 0 R
/E (The Brier score drops from 0.2051 to 0.0933 \(better than the base-rate reference of 0.0987\), and the ECE drops from 0.323 to 0.008)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
497 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1149 0 R
/E (The PR-AUC stays at 0.235, since the ranking is unchanged. Isotonic regression, by contrast, keeps only 78 distinct scores and lowers the PR-AUC to 0.228.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
498 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (The calibrated probabilities can now be trusted, with two cautions: they rarely exceed 30%, because the model separates patients only moderately, and calibration must be monitored over time, since it can break if the patient population changes.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
499 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (Calibrated probabilities also make the decision threshold interpretable. Indeed, our F1 threshold \(0.542\) corresponds to a 15% risk, which implicitly assumes that a missed readmission costs about 5.7 times more than an unnecessary follow-up. The threshold should be adjusted once the hospital defines its real costs.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
